            {
              "id": "09ee123e-29d2-3a0c-a7e9-b9047403f654",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the `strlen` function in C++. The `strlen` function is used to find the length of a string. However, it can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities if not used properly. This is because `strlen` does not check if the string is null-terminated. If a string is not null-terminated, `strlen` will continue reading memory until it encounters a null character, potentially leading to a buffer overflow.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always ensure that your strings are null-terminated. This can be done by initializing your strings with a null character or by manually adding a null character at the end of your strings. Additionally, you should always check the length of your strings before passing them to `strlen` to ensure that they do not exceed the size of your buffer.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the potential vulnerability lies in the `strlen(unit)` call. If `unit` is not null-terminated, this could lead to a buffer overflow. To fix this, you should ensure that `unit` is null-terminated and check its length before passing it to `strlen`. Here is a possible fix:\n\n```cpp\nif (unit != nullptr && strlen(unit) < BUFFER_SIZE) {\n    PrintColoredString(sout, get_log_color(COLOR_DEFAULT), \" %*s%s\", cNameLen - strlen(unit), s.c_str(), unit);\n}\n```\n\nIn this fix, `BUFFER_SIZE` is the size of your buffer. You should replace it with the actual size of your buffer.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet appears to depend on the following libraries:\n\n- `<cstring>` for the `strlen` function.\n- `<iostream>` for the `sout` stream.\n- A custom library for the `PrintColoredString` and `get_log_color` functions.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n\nPlease note that these links are subject to change and may not be available in the future."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "c6bd3e59-b907-3616-90f1-d27119874e03",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the `strlen` function in C++. The `strlen` function is used to find the length of a string. However, it can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities if not used properly. This is because `strlen` does not check if the string is null-terminated. If a string is not null-terminated, `strlen` will continue reading memory until it encounters a null character, potentially leading to a buffer overflow.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always ensure that your strings are null-terminated. This can be done by initializing your strings with a null character or by manually adding a null character at the end of your strings. Additionally, you should always check the length of your strings before passing them to `strlen` to ensure that they do not exceed the size of your buffer.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the potential vulnerability lies in the `strlen(unit)` call. If `unit` is not null-terminated, this could lead to a buffer overflow. To fix this, you should ensure that `unit` is null-terminated and check its length before passing it to `strlen`. Here is a possible fix:\n\n```cpp\nif (unit != nullptr && strlen(unit) < BUFFER_SIZE) {\n    PrintColoredString(sout, get_log_color(COLOR_DEFAULT), \" %*s%s\", cNameLen - strlen(unit), s.c_str(), unit);\n}\n```\n\nIn this fix, `BUFFER_SIZE` is the size of your buffer. You should replace it with the actual size of your buffer.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet appears to depend on the following libraries:\n\n- `<cstring>` for the `strlen` function.\n- `<iostream>` for the `sout` stream.\n- A custom library for the `PrintColoredString` and `get_log_color` functions.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n\nPlease note that these links are subject to change and may not be available in the future."
