            {
              "id": "f8ee7006-48e4-3ac3-b3b4-9769d6ed8efe",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc(n)` function in C++ is used to dynamically allocate memory at runtime. The function returns a pointer to the first byte of the allocated space if successful, or NULL if there is insufficient memory available. The vulnerability arises when the value of `n` is not properly validated or controlled, leading to potential buffer overflow, memory corruption, or other related issues.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to:\n\n1. Always validate and control the size of memory to be allocated.\n2. Check the return value of `malloc(n)` to ensure that the memory allocation was successful.\n3. Use memory management functions that initialize memory, such as `calloc()`, to prevent the use of uninitialized memory.\n4. Free the allocated memory after use to prevent memory leaks.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple example of how to properly use `malloc(n)`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\nint* allocateArray(int size) {\n    if (size <= 0) {\n        return NULL;\n    }\n\n    int* array = (int*) malloc(size * sizeof(int));\n\n    if (array == NULL) {\n        // Handle memory allocation failure\n        return NULL;\n    }\n\n    // Initialize array to zero\n    for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {\n        array[i] = 0;\n    }\n\n    return array;\n}\n\nvoid deallocateArray(int* array) {\n    free(array);\n}\n```\n\nIn this example, the size of the array to be allocated is validated and controlled. The return value of `malloc(n)` is checked to ensure that the memory allocation was successful. The allocated memory is initialized to zero to prevent the use of uninitialized memory. Finally, a function is provided to free the allocated memory after use.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `stdlib.h` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
